A||||5||fiero_hunter85||valve covers removal||||5||
Z||000000||fiero_hunter85||09-08-2001||07:31 PM||||anyone know how to or know a site that will tell me if and how i can remove the valve covers without taking off the intake manifold?||||reg||5
Z||000001||87GTZ34||09-08-2001||09:15 PM||||Some people have said it can be done.  When I was working on mine I didn't attempt to take them off w/o removing the intake as it looked like a very tight fit if they even would come off.<p>[This message has been edited by 87GTZ34 (edited 09-08-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||rogergarrison||09-08-2001||09:37 PM||||im assuming you have a V6. Id have to watch someone do it before id believe them. Maybe with the engine out, you could without taking off intake.||||reg||1
Z||000003||skitime||09-08-2001||10:03 PM||||I have taken valve covers off a Fiero V6 without touching the intake manifolds.  It is not easy and the rear one is worse. It seems impossible when you do it. They are hard to get off but somehow they always just seem to fall on.  Never figured out why or it would be easy both ways.<BR><P>------------------<BR><UL TYPE=SQUARE><BR><LI>Red 88 GT T-TOP<BR><LI>Red 86 GT<BR></UL> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/signature-1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||jelly2m8||09-08-2001||10:04 PM||||Yes the valve covers can come off without pulling the plenums, but it is a *****  of a job that way. The plenum gaskets are only about 15 bux canuk, so It doesnt really cost more to pull the upper and lower plenum off. <P>besides pulling the obvious, you need to remove the dogbone support on the rear bank, and the engine lift hook on the front bank.<P>You can pull the valve covers without even removing these 2 items, but you will have to work the valve covers off. Unless you have shitty looking valve covers, I recommend pulling the upper and lower plenum, along with the dogbone mount, and engine lift hook.  This way you wont mark up the finish on the valve covers.||||reg||1
Z||000005||2birds||09-08-2001||10:21 PM||||I just did it on mine two weeks ago.  Yee, it can be done, and yes, it is a tight fit.<P><BR>For the rear valve cover-<P>You have to bend the rear engine lift bracket to the left (a large screwdriver works just fine).<BR>You have to undo the dogbone and remove all but one of the bolts for the dogbone mounting bracket(the one on the cylinder head; leave the fore-to-aft dogbone mounting bracket attached by the bolt that it shares with the alternator bracket--loosen this bolt to give you some play.)<BR>Disconnect the weatherpack connector plugs at the coil/distributor(I removed the coil, too), and the temp sensor, and carefully work the flat wire bundle out between the intermediate part of the intake manifold and the valve cover, laying it to the right.  Now you can get to the valve cover bolts.  An 1/4" drive 8 mm socket with a swivel works well for the top two bolts, no swivel needed for the others.  The cover will now come out with a tiny bit of room to spare.<P><BR>For the front, it's easier with the trunk lid off.  Simple. one man job, just matchmark the four 13mm bolts/flatwashers to the hinges (I use Liquid Paper correction fluid), then remove them.  The lid isn't that heavy if you stand in the trunk.<BR>Then disconnect the weatherpack plugs from the TPS and IAC valve, the air inlet hose (better visibility) and breather line to the valve cover.  Unbolt the dipstick bracket from the forward engine lifting bracket (8mm again) and remove the lifting bracket.  Work the IAC/TPS wire bundle out the same way you did the aft bundle, and remove the valve cover the same way, too.<P>Beats buying an intake gasket set and having to clean/re-assemble everything.  You also take a chance at breaking your EGR tube  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"> and coolant leaks from the throttle body piping or broken vacuum lines.  Not to mention fuel injectors and fuel lines.||||reg||1
